david davis uh former brexit secretary  uh who is the conservative mp  for haltom price and howden is with us  now hello to you mr dave it's great  to talk to you you're one of the rebels  are you going to win  well i think so uh certainly if uh all  those who want to vote for us  are allowed to we will win um i'm pretty  confident we'll win  whatever the whips try and do if if the  if the  amendment is called of course it changed  your tune though haven't you because you  didn't used to agree  with the 0.7 gdp i've got a quote from  you here well i don't actually agree  with the government this is in 2012.  the government's policy on uh the 0.7  i think that is just nonsense we should  spend  the money on aid that needs to be spent  on aid why have you changed your mind  well what i said at the time and you're  quite right as a public accounts  committee chairman  i said this is too fast an increase it  would lead to inefficiencies  but what we're looking at today is cuts  that will be massive cuts  in provision of clean water dirty water  kills more children than probably  anything else in the world other than  malaria maybe  we're seeing massive cuts to food  support about a quarter of a million  people 250 000 people will be  facing starvation without that what  we're talking about and big cuts to the  biggest emergency areas in the world one  of your  speakers earlier talked about the  the reduction to yemen the biggest  emergency with the world has  all of these things will lead to loss of  life probably tens of thousands of  children will die  as a result of these cuts nobody else  amongst the g7 is doing this uh germany  now has caught up with 0.7  france will uh pass that uh this year as  well  the americans are increasing their  spending by 16 billion  a year all of them face similar economic  issues to us  we've actually got the fastest recovery  economic recovery rate  uh in the modern world uh and and as a  result  why are we doing this thing which will  lead to the deaths of large numbers of  children  but richie sunak would say in response  to that that the economic outlook  is still uncertain at this stage we  can't  afford it when we can it's only  temporary when we can afford it again  then of course we'll put it back to 0.7  and it does look as though the public  agrees 60 percent of the public when  they were asked said yep  charity starts at home well it depends  how you  how you put the question to them if you  tell them the consequence will be tens  of thousands of children will die i  think you get a rather different answer  if you say oh should we spend on  something at home first  of course you'll get that answer the  simple truth is  that this is not necessary it's less  than one percent of the borrowings that  uh that risk is going in for this year  and as a result it doesn't make economic  sense and as you've heard from lisa  nandy and others  it will undermine our position a very  very hard  one position uh with so-called soft  power our respect in the world if you  like  it will undermine that too but for me  the issue is about lives  children's lives and frankly even if it  was an unpopular policy i would still  stand up for it  i don't want to be a member of a  government or a supporter of a  government  that is effectively deciding to uh  lead to tens of thousands of deaths of  small children  and it does appear that many  backbenchers agree  with you i think 45 need  to vote against the government in or in  favor of the amendment in order  to change the direction of this uh uk  foreign aid to that end i believe that  the chancellor's been ringing people  uh over the weekend mps personally  um to ask whether they will change their  minds did he contact you  no he didn't um he probably realized it  would be it would be a futile exercise  but that tells you  uh that we are in winning territory  because you know the chancellor doesn't  spend his weekend calling out back  ventures normally  uh and and we are in winning territory  if this is put to the vote i believe  we'll win  and the government will have to defend  this policy and that will save lives  yeah embarrassing for the prime minister  to potentially lose  a vote uh in his own house of commons  ahead of  the g7 he's going to have a bloody nose  isn't he heading down to cornwall  yeah well i'm sorry about that but you  know i i really don't want to see him  embarrassed nobody does actually  when we're when we're hosting g7 but the  simple truth is that this should have  been brought to the house so this was in  our manifesto  uh the 0.7 we were the leaders in the  world doing it  uh and it should have if he wanted to  change it he should have brought it to  the house of commons and allowed the  house to rule on it  uh i think actually he would have lost  and we would still a bit 0.7 today if  he'd done that which is why he didn't do  it  and as a result what he's got is a  policy that the uh the  one of the best directors of public  prosecutions in modern times  uh ken mcdonald said is unlawful so  we've got an unlawful policy we need to  put right  if he'd if he'd come to the house first  we would have corrected at the beginning  unfortunately now we've got to do it  when we can do it which is today  one of the other issues he's going to  have to deal with of course is the dog's  dinner that is the  uh border in the irish sea we heard from  the president you were brexit secretary  of the  president brexit minister saying oh it's  harder than we  anticipated uh it was going to be  despite three former  prime minister as uh george mitchell the  cbi so many others explaining that  let me just play this clip to you if  you'll indulge me uh from the uh  government minister this morning when i  put that to her  lord frost is absolutely trying to get  it sorted and he was in ireland last  week  and doing exactly that it is very  difficult on the ground  in terms of trade um and you know it is  important that we ensure that we do have  uh trade between um between the uk  and and uh ireland between between  england and ireland  and it is really important that we saw  that and lord frost is doing exactly  that but you understand  that it does look as though you  underestimated the complexities  of this agreement well as it has panned  out on the ground it is more difficult  than we anticipated and we do need to  sort out that trade arrangement  we're going to be paying the price for  many years to come because our trade is  affected  imports and exports are affected  northern ireland is particularly  affected  and i really hope the government can  sort out some of these problems as  quickly as possible  because it's about jobs it's about  livelihoods it's about the supply of  food and everything else it's got to be  sorted out  mr davis to what extent do you accept  responsibility for this mess  well it's one of the things i resigned  over you may remember  i did predict to the prime minister at  the time when she conceded the so-called  full alignment wording  that this was problematic not what we  promised  and would lead to difficulties in the  future and that's exactly what we're  seeing as i say that's why i resigned  uh and after that checkers meeting a  couple of years back  isn't that i suppose my question is  shouldn't you have stayed to try to sort  it out  it wasn't possible once you got to the  point of uh  agreeing the alignment of northern irish  regulations with southern irish  regulations you are creating a border of  one sort or another you're creating a  border  which would end up falling in the irc  would be much simpler to create an  invisible border  between the north and south of ireland  that was the original policy  that's what theresa may abandoned i'm  afraid and that as i say in due course  led to me resigning which was  the only way to break the only way to  bring it to an end frankly because  as it was her cabinet was determined to  carry on with this policy which would  lead to where we are today  it will be resolved it's difficult and  it but it will be resolved because  everybody wants to resolve it  but it's an unnecessary debt difficulty  and it had  a couple of years of negotiation to the  overall  outcome that was going to be my question  what what is the resolution how do you  see it being resolved because of course  in the wings  we've got the leaders of free world  saying unless you sort that out  forget a trade deal with the united  states  well the the secret goes way way back if  you if you cast your mind back  right the beginning of the negotiations  both the  irish uh head of taxation the head of  their equivalent of the hmrc  and our head of hmrc you said it was  perfectly possible  to manage with an invisible border  between the north and the south  under different arrangements uh and  similarly  you know with technical mechanisms you  can do the same here trusted trader  arrangements  uh agreed agreed regulatory arrangements  that sort of thing  it's possible to do it it would just  take a lot of detail and it'll take  i'm guessing frankly because i'm no  longer that close to the  negotiation but i think probably a  couple of years of detailed  onward negotiation to facilitate all of  these things  going back and forth it shouldn't have  been necessary uh it shouldn't have  happened but it happened because  uh we conceded to an aggressive uh  eu stance on this which when we  shouldn't have done it but it's not it's  not insoluble  it's practically resolvable but it'll  take a year or two  and you're pleased it's not you having  to fix it  no i'm not pleased it's not me having a  picture that isn't why i resigned i  resigned to make a point  to actually bring to a to crystallize if  you like the problem  the outcome was the government changed  boris came in  we got the outcome we got which is  slightly better than what was there  before  and we will get a resolution eventually  that's that's that's  that's that's worth a resignation even  my own  david davis great to talk to you thanks  for joining us this morning

